  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last year, the amount of money spent on lobbying \nhit a record $5.24 billion at the federal level  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alone. And well, I don't know if you realized, but \nthere weren't even any major federal elections in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2025. When you factor in the upcoming midterms, \nstate and local level lobbying in addition to all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the money spent outside of officially disclosed \nchannels, the volume of cash that is uh enabling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lawmaking process is going to eclipse 11 \nfigures within America alone this year. And by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all accounts, it's going to be a great investment. \nA Cambridge University report found that out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1,779 policy issues passed during their study, \nthe preferences of economic elites and special  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "business interests had a significant influence on \npolicy outcomes, which wasn't really the shocking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part. The shocking part was that the preferences \nof average citizens had effectively no measurable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence, even in large groups that were \nextremely politically active. Oh, and this study  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was done in 2014 when total political spending \nwas less than a quarter of what it is today.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Part of the reason why that spending has become so \nludicrous is not because the money is being used  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to circumvent the will of the people. That battle \nwas won long ago. It's instead becoming a spending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "battle between other lobbyists to see whose \nspecial interest takes precedence over the lesser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "donors. Now, I know the problem of political \nlobbying is not exactly a surprise to anybody  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anymore. Even here on this channel, whenever we \ncover broken business models, from private equity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private credit, corporate farming subsidies, \nthe gambling epidemic, data center subsidies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92960,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corporate consolidation, healthcare, the \nhousing market, how you fund a retirement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and all the way down to something as mundane as \nhow we collect economic data. Political lobbying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is at least part of the reason why things have \nbecome so clearly messed up. But I promise you,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "however bad you think it's gotten, it's worse. \nWe tend to think that money in politics begins  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113040,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ends with how much our elected officials \ncan pump into silly campaign ads and how many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lobbyists and industry group can pay to stock \nthe halls of the capital. But even if we were to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somehow reform this in its entirety tomorrow, we \nonly need to look to other countries to see it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably not going to fix all of our problems. Oh, \nand of course, this is all assuming that everybody  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was actually playing by those rules in the first \nplace. But when it comes to money and politics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138640,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which groups give the most? In 2025, Open AAI and \nAnthropic, valued at 830 billion and $380 billion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "respectively, escalated their federal lobbying \nefforts. Hospitals, insurers, doctors, unions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pharmaceutical companies spent a record \n$19 million lobbying Beacon Hill lawmakers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last year. National Association of Realtors at 54 \nmillion. The business roundt at 27. The American  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "political system is broken. It is corrupt. \nIt is significantly owned by the billionaire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "class. Okay, so there's a lot of money going \ninto American politics. I know that alone isn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surprising, but the headline spending numbers \nadding up to a mere 10 billion in change are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really just the basic prices. The actual bill \nis a lot bigger, and most of it never shows up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the receipt of public disclosure. To see \nthis, we should start with the most obvious  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sources of money in finance and then work \nour way down to the most discreet. So yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kicking off with direct campaign contributions. \nMost people think that money in politics is only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "writing big checks to politicians so they can fund \ntheir increasingly expensive political campaigns.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now elections have absolutely become more \nexpensive. Advertising directed by staffers is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost unavoidable at this point, and presidential \ncandidates in particular are increasingly flying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private and running huge teams to manage longer \nand more grandiose campaigns. But despite what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it may look like, there are actually pretty strict \nlimits on how much any individual person can give  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to these candidates. Currently, the Federal \nElection Commission cap on contributions to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any federal level elected official, including \nthe president, is $3,500 per election. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the primary and the general election, meaning \nthe absolute most a single person can give to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a candidate in one election cycle is $7,000. \nThe rules on this are strict and they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually enforced. Additionally, individual \ncontributors have to be real natural people. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you are thinking you could just route your \ncontributions through a thousand shell companies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "congratulations. You are starting to think like a \npolitical lobbyist. But in this particular case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is not allowed. Now, $7,000 from an \nindividual is a lot of money for most people,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271120,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in the grand scheme of most election \ncampaigns, it is really just a drop in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bucket and is unlikely to be enough on its \nown to sway any policy at this level. You may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "occasionally hear the phrase, \"I can't believe how \nlittle Congress people will sell themselves out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for when news breaks that a couple paid $10,000 \nto a campaign and it looks like they got tailored  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "legislative treatment.\" In reality, that is \nnormally just what is being disclosed through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "official campaign contributions. And there is \nnormally a lot more money involved behind the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scenes. Direct contributions to a single campaign \nare just the most obvious, most regulated, most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reported, and arguably least problematic way that \nmoney gets into politics. Most people can outright  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$3,500 checks every election cycle. But enough \npeople do make contributions that it is very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difficult for any single wealthy donor to stand \nout based on their direct spending alone. Now, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one exception to the FEC cap is that candidates \ncan put as much of their own money as they want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into their own campaign. And significantly wealthy \nfigures from both sides of the aisle like Michael  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bloomberg and Donald Trump have both campaigned \non their ability to do this. Bloomberg put roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$936 million of his own money into his \nunsuccessful 2020 presidential campaign,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which lasted about a 100 days, making it by far \nthe most expensive per day campaign in American  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history. Trump contributed over $56 million into \nhis own 2016 campaign and made it a big point that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this would separate him from other politicians who \nneed to keep their donors happy. Though, according  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to public disclosures, he didn't contribute \nany of his own money in 2020 or 2024. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get used to hearing this, but even through direct \ncontributions, there are still some loopholes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Individual contribution limits are $7,000, but \nthere is a very popular workaround called a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "joint fundraising committee. Not to be confused \nwith my fellow broke dorm room friends back in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "college who went by the same name. These allow \na single donor to write one enormous check that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could split across a candidate, a national party, \nand multiple state parties all at once, filling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up the limits across all of these categories \nin a single transaction. In the 2024 cycle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single donor could legally contribute over \n$800,000 through one of these arrangements.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not great, but this alone is far from the \nbiggest reason why money in politics has gotten  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so out of control. The $7,000 cap only applies to \ndirect contributions. And even with a multiple six  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figure workaround, this money pales in comparison \nto what is now being contributed very openly by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wealthy individuals through super PACs which have \nno cap at all. Now, packs or political action  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "committees are organizations that can support \na specific interest, like a political campaign,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and were really just meant as a way for supporters \nto organize and make their voices collectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heard. Regular packs also have limits on how \nmuch they are allowed to spend. Super PACs are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technically distinct from regular packs. Legally, \nthey aren't actually technically packs at all.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, they are officially classified as \nindependent expenditure only committees, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might sound like nerdy legal technicalities, but \nit means that they can take in unlimited amounts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of money and spend unlimited amounts of money on \nany issue they see fit, including the election of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "individuals or the passing of specific laws. This \nfreedom to spend all they want comes on the one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specific condition that they do not directly \ncoordinate with the political campaign that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are supporting. And even this lowest of bars \ngets pretty openly ignored. These were the groups  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that were enabled by the Citizens United ruling in \n2010, which established that political spending is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protected free speech under the First Amendment. \nThe idea is that people with specific political  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "concerns should be allowed to use their free \nspeech to push a political agenda and spending is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of that free speech. But to avoid it becoming \njust an endound campaign finance limits, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be no direct coordination with the campaign \nitself. This is broken pretty regularly and to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nobody's surprise, pretty flagrantly, too. Elon \nMusk donated roughly $239 million to America Pack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2024 to support Donald Trump's presidential \ncampaign, making it one of the largest super PAC  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513120,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "operations in history. And the no cooperation rule \nwas uh well, the FC actually issued a ruling that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowed super PACs to coordinate directly with \ncampaigns on door-to-door canvasing operations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which America pack immediately took advantage of. \nThe campaign's own political director publicly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "credited Musk's operation with conserving campaign \nfunds by outsourcing field operations to the super  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "PAC, which allowed Trump's campaign to redirect \nmoney into ad spending. Now, spending money on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ads alone is obviously a big deal that can move \nelections. Even here on YouTube, the amount of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money we make on the platform before major federal \nelections increases significantly because of how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much is being spent on endless campaign ads. But \nthese messages don't actually require that much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordination. The formula for political ads isn't \nexactly that hard to reverse engineer. Say bad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things about the other guy and nice things \nabout your guy. which means in this space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most super PACs find it a lot easier to keep \ntheir legally mandated safe distance. But it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also not the only place where they spend their \nmoney. Data collection and processing is a huge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of modern political campaigns. It's also \nreally expensive and a lot harder to hand over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without crossing the line of direct collaboration. \nBut even here, there are plenty of groups that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uh bend these rules. When JD Vance was first \nrunning for senator in my home state of Ohio,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he was heavily supported by one of Peter Teal's \nsuper PACs named Protect Ohio Values. It turns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out Teal has a thing for spending lots of money \nto collect data on potential voters. Anyway,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the only problem was he couldn't just hand that \ndata over to Vance's campaign without it breaking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the arms distance rule of super PACs. So instead, \nthey just uploaded the data to an obscure blog for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his campaign team to stumble upon with the hope \nthat nobody else would find it. tucked away in an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obscure corner of the internet. This was of course \nchallenged for being a pretty blatant flouting of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rules, but the case was ultimately dismissed. \nNow, of course, hopefully it should go without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "saying that this happens just as much on both \nsides of the aisle. Democratic Alliance super PACs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spent comparable amounts in 2024. Future Forward, \na pro Harris super PAC, raised over $700 million,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their coordination was probably a little \nbit too close for the spirit of these rules as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well. In total, outside spending by super PACs and \nother independent groups exceeded $4.5 billion in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the 2024 cycle, more than double what it was in \n2016. Collectively, super PACs alongside direct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "campaign finance is likely to exceed $10 billion \nat the federal level this year. It's not great,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but at least we know it's actually happening \nbecause, well, the money we can actually see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is far from the biggest problem. So, it's time \nto learn how money works to find out just how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deep this goes, why even the most aggressive \ncampaign finance reform probably can't fix it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and why nobody really seems to want to try. If you \nhave watched this channel for any amount of time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you already know that most companies do not \nactually care about your privacy. They sell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your data, they lose your data, and they treat it \nlike a line item on a balance sheet. Which is why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this video is sponsored by Cape. We have worked \nwith Cape before, and they have shipped a few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new features worth catching you up on. Cape is \na privacy focused mobile carrier that gives you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unlimited talk, text, and data, plus a stack of \nsecurity features no other carrier includes. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recently rolled out an IMSI rotation, which swaps \nthe static subscriber ID your phone broadcast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every 24 hours, so carriers and advertisers cannot \ntrack you in real time. They have also launched  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "secondary numbers, two extra real phone lines \nbuilt into your plan, so you can hand them out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for banking or shopping and keep your main line \nprivate. And finally, disappearing call logs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which autodeletes your call and text metadata \nafter 24 hours, so there is nothing to leak  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or sell. Use code how many works 33 for 33% off \nyour first 6 months of Cape. Cape is available on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apple, Google Play, and the Acrescent store. Link \nin my description. Okay, so campaign donations and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super PACs are really just scratching the surface \nof these issues. The problem is that when you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look deeper, it gets much worse. The first level \ndown is what is not at all sinisterly known as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark money. Dark money is politically motivated \nspending that gets redirected through various  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organizations to push an agenda without any clear \ndisclosure and without any clear source. These  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are typically structured as 501c4 nonprofits \nwhich under IRS rules are allowed to engage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in political activity so long as it is not their \nprimary purpose. This means they can spend up to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "49% of their budget on politics and never have \nto disclose a single donor. The Brennan Center  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "estimates that over $1 billion in dark money was \nspent in federal elections between 2010 and 2024.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And those are just the expenditures that could be \ntracked. And well, by definition, the thing that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes dark money dark is that most of it can't \nbe. A major example that we have covered just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recently is Build America AI which is a dark money \norganization pushing the idea that America needs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to beat China and AI which very clearly feeds \ninto the business interests of hyperscalers and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI labs that need red tape cut land allotted for \ntheir data centers and maybe some bailout money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in case investor hype dies down. Now, obviously \nthis is not great, but at least in this case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their completely transparent motives make them \npretty transparent, but the whole point of a dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money group is that you cannot trace the money \nback to the people who benefit from the policy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes. And this has a few other advantages, too. \nUnder federal law, foreign nationals are banned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from donating to American political campaigns or \npacks. But because 501c4 nonprofits don't have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to disclose their donors, there is effectively no \nmechanism to enforce this for dark money groups.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A foreign government or foreign corporation can \ndonate to a 501c4 which then spends that money on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "American ads with a political agenda and nobody \nwould ever know. The FEC has acknowledged this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loophole exists. Yet they have not closed it. \nAnd then there are the corporate interests that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just throw away the middleman altogether. There \nis really nothing stopping a major company from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just running its own ads pushing a very specific \npolitical message. This is happening a lot at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moment with companies claiming that their AI data \ncenters are going to be great for local economies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "running ads in swing districts about job creation \nand infrastructure investment that look a lot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like political endorsements but technically \naren't. So when work started to slow down,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we searched for new opportunities and we welcome \nMeta which opened a data center in our town.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So long as they avoid FEC trigger words like vote \nfor or vote against and don't mention specific  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "legislation, it's really hard to regulate this as \npolitical spending over a company just expressing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its own interests as protected speech. But it gets \nworse. Everything we just covered, all of that is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really just the price of admission. The thing that \nactually moves the needle once the politicians are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Washington is lobbying. And lobbying is a very \ndifferent animal from campaign spending. Lobbyists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are in theory just concerned citizens with very \nspecific interests about very specific policies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality, they are primarily highly paid field \nexperts from elite law firms funded by industry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "groups to support a very specific agenda. Now, to \nplay devil's advocate a little bit here, a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lawmakers are not experts in the things they are \ncreating laws on. So, being able to consult with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949200,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a diverse pool of industry specialists could \ntheoretically avoid designing laws that are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just dumb for their specific application. Laws \non technology probably shouldn't be exclusively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "written by an 80-year-old senator who doesn't know \nthe difference between a coding language and their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high school transcripts. And that kind of makes \nsense. But the problem is that almost all of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work of actually drafting legislation gets passed \noff to these lobbyists who get to word things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exactly how they want and ensure it won't get held \nup in courts because they are the legal experts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Congressional staffers have openly described how \nlobbyists will hand them fully written legislative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "text ready to be introduced. And because the \nstaffers are overworked and underresourced,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they will sometimes file it with barely any \nchanges. So really, in a lot of cases, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lobbying firms are the lawmakers. The lawmakers \nthemselves are just the rubber stamp. Now, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the widely held assumption that this corporate \nfunded lobbying is working exclusively to cut red  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tape and remove regulations. In reality, it \nis often exactly the opposite. They want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "introduce regulation in exactly the right areas \nto block potential new challengers from entering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their market. The pharmaceutical industry is \nparticularly good at this. They spent over $370  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "million on lobbying in 2024 alone, more than \nany other industry that year, and a huge chunk  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that went towards ensuring that drug pricing \nregulations are written in a way that protects  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their existing patent portfolios while making \nit harder for generic competitors to enter the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market. The old team at Micro did a great video \non this, explaining how internet providers lobbied  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against municipalities looking to roll out their \nown broadband and actually deliver a competitive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "service. In both cases, getting the right kind \nof laws passed was a lot more profitable than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having no regulations at all. There are now over \n14,000 registered lobbyists in Washington alone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which works out to roughly 26 lobbyists for every \nsingle elected official in Congress. And if you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "include the people doing lobbying adjacent work \nwho don't formally register, some estimates put  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real number closer to 100,000. And all of this \nis just the federal level. State legislatores are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arguably worse because they get a fraction of the \nmedia scrutiny. State level lobbying disclosure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requirements are weaker, staffs are smaller, \nand individual lobbyists can have an outsized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence on a state representative who might only \nhave one or two aids and no research budget to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independently verify what the lobbyist is telling \nthem. Of course, these people also coordinate the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "efforts of everything we've already talked about. \nThey can suggest that a given company or industry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "group would really like this type of legislation \nand that conveniently they are looking to spend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of money advertising this issue. A study \nlooking at 93 multinational corporations that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lobbyed for a single tax provision, the American \nJobs Creation Act of 2004, found that those firms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spent a combined $283 million on lobbying \nand received $62.5 billion in tax savings,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "working out to a return of $220 for every single \ndollar spent. Those returns have to come from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somewhere, and that somewhere is the tax base, the \nconsumer or the competitor who got regulated out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the market. Now, there is one other reason why \nlobbying has become such an effective tool against  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1132720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lawmakers. And this other reason also explains \none of the other logical questions you may have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had so far, which is why do lawmakers let this \nhappen? Yes, they want their re-election funding,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there are alternative systems to get it. And \nby just taking a step back if nobody else had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lobbyists to answer to or super PAC money funding \ntheir campaigns, it means they wouldn't need it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either. They could change these laws because, \nwell, that's literally their job. And so far,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it looks like the only reason they don't want to \nis because the other guy won't do it first. Beyond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164400,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weird prisoners dilemma they have backed \nthemselves into here, it must be recognized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that for these lawmakers constantly begging for \ncampaign funding and playing nice with an army of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lobbyists telling them how to do their job has got \nto be pretty demeaning. So even if they don't do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it for the good of their constituents, surely they \nwould be motivated to reform these laws just to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make their own job less humiliating, right? Well, \nthere is one good reason why they don't, and that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1188640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the revolving door. Elected officials at the \nfederal level are paid well by the standards of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1194160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average Americans, but not by the standards of \nthe people they interact with on a daily basis.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A member of Congress earns $174,000 a year. The \nmedian partner at a top lobbying firm in DC earns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1205760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well over a million. A huge unspoken way that \nmoney gets into politics is the promise of a cushy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "job for when they retire or get voted out. This \nextends well beyond elected officials as well.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1216880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our video on corporate consolidation, we found \nthat almost all of the previous FTC chairpersons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1221440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the last two decades, with the exception of \nLena Khan, went on to get high-profile roles at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "M\u0026A law firms, specifically working on the kinds \nof deals that the FTC should probably be blocking.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1231760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, the person whose job it is to block \nanti-competitive mergers knows that in 2 years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1236640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they will probably be working for the firms doing \nthose mergers. That creates an incentive that no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1241200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of campaign finance reform can fix. \nNow the number one most common career move  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1245920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for elected officials after leaving politics is \nto become a political lobbyist. In 2025 alone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "866 members of Congress and congressional staffers \nmade the move from Capitol Hill to K Street,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1257600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a 60% increase over the year before, and \na new record. In total, roughly half of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1262560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all former lawmakers end up working as political \nlobbyists. Jack Abramoff, one of the few lobbyists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1267920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who has actually been convicted for pushing these \nflexible laws too far, put it pretty bluntly when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1272400,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he admitted that he would tell a congressional \nstaffer, \"When you're done working on the Hill,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1276160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we'd very much like you to consider coming \nto work for us.\" And that was most of his  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1280480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "job done. Every request from our office, every \nrequest of our clients, everything that we want,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1285600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're going to do. And not only that, they're \ngoing to think of things we can't think of to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1290080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it. Former representatives and staffers are the \nperfect candidates to become lobbyists because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1294720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they know their way around the capital, but also \nbecause they are a walking billboard showing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1299200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every current staffer that playing along with the \nlobbyists is the path to a seven figure salary as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1304720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lobbyist. Now, to be fair, there are cooling \noff periods in place. Former members of Congress  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1310000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are technically barred from lobbying for 1 to two \nyears after leaving office. But these rules are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1314640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trivally easy to get around. You can do strategic \nconsulting or government relations advisory work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1320000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is, and I cannot stress this enough, exactly \nthe same thing without technically registering as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1325280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lobbyist until this period is up. And well, even \nif you did somehow fully enforce the cooling off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1330880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "period and close every loophole, it still wouldn't \nfix the core problem because the incentive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1335680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is baked into the system the moment a staffer \nrealizes that the people they are writing laws for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1340240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are also the people who are going to sign their \nnext paycheck. But the revolving door at least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1344960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "involves people who used to work in government. \nThe next channel of influence doesn't even require  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1349840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that. Obviously, certain owners of major news \noutlets have certain political leanings, and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1354400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can put weight on candidates to support their \nincentives or face unfavorable coverage. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1359120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this one is really hard to quantify because media \ninfluence doesn't show up in any spending report.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1364240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is no FC filing for choose not to cover the \nstory or ran 17 segments on this topic instead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1370080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that one. And no, this is not a segue to a \nground news ad, but the effects are very real.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1375920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The media landscape has become so partisan that \nthis is arguably even more important in primaries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1380560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than in general elections because the threat of \npoor coverage from a politically aligned outlet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1385200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can end a primary campaign before it starts. If \nyou are running as a Republican and can't get onto  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1390400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fox News, you're kind of cooked. Hypothetically, \nof course, social media platforms are now owned by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1396160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people with very specific political and business \ninterests. and the editorial decisions about what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1400720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gets amplified, what gets suppressed, and what \ngets labeled as misinformation are being made by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1405440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same people who are pushing for specific \npolicy outcomes. Even companies that are not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1409840,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traditional media can also influence this by \nsuggesting they would feel more comfortable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1413520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advertising on networks that did or did not push a \ncertain political agenda. I can say from personal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1418720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experience that even down here at Little Works \nMedia Group, we have lost sponsors because we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1422880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pointed out things like the lunacy surrounding \ncurrent AI spending and the push for bailouts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1427520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is becoming a very real pressure even at \nthis level. And for better or worse, influencers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1432480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and independent creators are becoming a political \nforce. And then there's the one that nobody really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1437440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to talk about for very long because it is \nexactly what it sounds like, straight up bribes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1443760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, this kind of direct corruption isn't as \nnecessary as it used to be because of all of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1448080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other legal channels we just went through, \nbut it does still happen and enforcement is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1452720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to put it generously, relaxed. Senator Bob Mendez \nwas convicted on 16 federal charges in 2024 for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1459040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accepting gold bars, cash, and a Mercedes-Benz in \nexchange for official acts, and his colleagues in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1464240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Senate only moved to expel him after the \nconviction, at which point he resigned before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1468720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a vote could take place. And Menendez isn't \nalone. In 2024, Business Insider found that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1474320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over 100 members of Congress or their spouses made \nstock trades and industries directly affected by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1478880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their committee work. A senator sitting on the \nArmed Services Committee buying defense stocks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1483280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before a major procurement announcement is well \nexactly what it looks like. And the stock act  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1488720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that was supposed to prevent this is enforced \nwith fines of $200. $200 on a trade that could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1494400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "net hundreds of thousands is not a deterrent. The \nuncomfortable truth is that when you line up all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1499520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seven of these channels together, the direct \ncontributions, the super PACs, the dark money,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1503680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the corporate ads, the lobbyists, the revolving \ndoor, the media influence, and yeah, the bribes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1508720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what you are looking at is a system where the \nlegal options are so effective that the illegal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1512800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ones are almost redundant. Now, some people have \naccused me of being a bit of a gloomy goose. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1519440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to talk about some actual genuine real \ngood news here. At a state level, there has been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1524480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some progress to circumvent the super PAC funding \nenabled by the Citizens United ruling in several  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1529040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states. And at least in state legislatores, it has \nreceived unanimous support from both sides of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1533840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aisle. This won't fix all of these problems, and \nthe legal techniques they are using won't work at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1538880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the federal level without a new Supreme Court \nruling, but this is still a step in the right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1543440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction. Like always, the big Blizzard beat me \nto covering this particular topic, so I will leave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1547920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a link to his video in the description alongside \nsome other articles. Unfortunately, the federal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1552560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level is going to need a little bit more work. \nThe obvious fix is to close the revolving door,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1557120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remove the financial incentive for lawmakers to \nplay along, and maybe hot tickler actually pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1562000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elected officials enough that a lobbying salary \nisn't a six-fold raise. Yes, that would cost more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1567840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money in government salaries, but it would be a \ndrop in the bucket compared to how much money is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1571920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lost through poor policy. Those 93 companies \nthat spent $283 million on lobbying and got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1577680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$62.5 billion back did not create that money out \nof thin air. That money came from somewhere. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1583920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that somewhere is the tax base, workers paychecks, \nand consumers wallets. There have been efforts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1589920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The For the People Act passed in the House in 2019 \nand again in 2021 with provisions to require dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1595600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money disclosures and tighten revolving door \nrestrictions. It died in the Senate both times.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1601040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Disclose Act, which would have required 501c4 \ngroups to name donors who give them over $10,000,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1606720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been introduced in every Congress since 2010. \nIt has never passed. But the only people who could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1613280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pass these reforms are the exact same people \nwho benefit from the current system. You are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1617920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentially asking politicians to vote themselves \na stricter set of rules and a permanent ban on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1622320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "career they were planning to retire into. Now, \nif you want to see all these systems actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1626720,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "put together, go and watch this next video \nto find out why we are already subsidizing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1630480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI data centers. And don't forget to like and \nsubscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
}
